The committed budgets are contract inputs, not measurements from a developer
workstation. `ledger-full.txt` and `ledger-minimal.txt` were generated by the
pinned Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64-linux-gnu measurement described by Sprint 57:
GCC 13.3.0 and GNU binutils 2.42. `make size-check` regenerates and
byte-compares both files; `make size-update` is the explicit rebaseline path.

The absolute gate measures `stat -c %s` of the stripped file produced by the
Linux `SHIPPING=1` profile: `-O2`, `-DNDEBUG`, function/data sections, linker
section garbage collection, no build id, and no unwind tables.  The last four
choices affect only the shipping profile; ordinary, sanitizer, and
`perf-symbols` builds retain their normal debugging and profiling metadata.
`GC_SECTIONS=0` exists only to measure and record the linker-GC delta. The
committed ledgers name that delta as `gc_saved_bytes`.

Sprint 57 amendment S57-A1 sets the achievable hard bars at 2 MiB full and
1.5 MiB minimal. The amendment is based on the pinned x86_64 GCC/glibc
measurement plus an independent native x86_64 musl rebuild; in both minimal
profiles `.text + .rodata` alone exceeded the old whole-file cap. The former
1.5 MiB full / 900 KiB minimal planning values remain post-1.0 optimization
ratchets. They are not alternate measurements and must not be selected by a
CI flag.

The four single-module values are the Sprint 57 contract caps. Their `why`
fields record the pinned GCC build's on-disk delta over minimal, rounded
upward to the next 10 KiB. A local developer build is not acceptable
provenance for an update.

`make size-check` also drives a clean PTY open for the shipping full and
minimal binaries. `scripts/size-memory.sh` reads the `rss checkpoint=paint`
rows and enforces a peak no greater than 1.6 times the byte size of
`100m-code`; the minimal build additionally enforces a 6 MiB empty-buffer
first-paint ceiling. `make size-musl` applies both gates to the static musl
binary. Missing or malformed checkpoints are harness errors, never passes.

Size gates do not authorize feature removal. Core Yew, its invariants, and
the required minimal-build feature matrix remain fixed inputs. When their
measured implementation floor conflicts with a planning number, the work
stops for an evidence-backed amendment or deferral; the measurement must not
be made green by gutting behavior, weakening stability, or changing what is
counted.
